*difficulties, & respecting each other’s rights & liberties, which is the foundation of the structure.*
>>>

<<<
It may fairly be said that the sole function of mutual(ly) is to give the sense of some part of each other when it happens to be hard to get each other into one’s sentence; if each other not only can be, but is, got in, m. is superfluous;
===
It may fairly be said that the sole function of *mutual(ly)* is to give the sense of some part of *each other* when it happens to be hard to get *each other* into one’s sentence; if *each other* not only can be, but is, got in, *m.* is superfluous;
>>>

<<<
follow:—The ring was mutually chosen by the Duke & Lady Elisabeth last Wednesday./ They have affinities beyond a m. admiration for Mazzini./M. exchange
===
follow:—*The ring was mutually chosen by the Duke & Lady Elisabeth last Wednesday./ They have affinities beyond a m. admiration for Mazzini./M. exchange*
